  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery early this morning shows a shortwave trough\nover the central Rockies. This feature is progged to crest the\nupper ridge over the central/southern High Plains today before\nturning southeast and amplifying as it moves into the Midwest\nand mid-Mississippi Valley Friday into Friday night. The 00z \nruns of the GFS and ECMWF both close off a 500mb low Friday \nnight into Saturday morning, supporting an unusually deep \nsurface low for this time of year. \n\nEven in the farther north ECMWF solution, the warm sector and\nstronger instability remain mostly south of our CWA. Impressive\nupper level divergence with some coupling of upper level jet\nstreaks will enhance ascent in a developing deformation zone on\nthe north side of this cyclone. PWATs at or above 150% of \nnormal, coupled with weak static stability, and strong ascent \npoint to the potential for heavy rainfall within the deformation\nband on the north side of this cyclone. Can't rule out a few \nembedded thunderstorms given the weak elevated instability, \nparticularly with parcels being lifted isentropically, but even \nwithout thunder, it still looks favorable for potentially \nconvective rainfall rates at times Friday night into Saturday \nmorning. \n\nThere's still some spread where exactly the deformation band and\nheaviest rainfall totals will set up, but potential exists for\nwidespread rainfall totals over an inch with localized swaths of\n2\"+ totals. Flash flood guidance is pretty high over most of the\nCWA, though lower in the Chicago metro area where Monday storms\nhave left soils more saturated and streams more elevated.\nCertainly possible that any transient f-gen driven mesoscale \nbands of heavier rain/isolated thunderstorms could result in \na localized flash flood threat, particularly if it were to \noccur in the Chicago metropolitan area where antecedent \nconditions are wetter. Later shifts may need to consider an ESF \nor possibly even a flood watch, but enough spread in guidance at\nthis point to justify holding off. \n\nThere's also spread in guidance with how quickly this system\nand its associated rain move out of the area Saturday. NBM pops\nlook pretty reasonable with highest pops in the morning trending\ndown in the afternoon. This upper low is progged to be shoved\nsouth of the area as upper ridge flops eastward across the Great\nLakes Sunday into the start of next week. That should result in\na return to dry conditions and seasonably warm temperatures. \n\n- Izzi",
